 {"n": "Little Yosemite Valley", "u": 0.4772, "v": 0.66924, "k": "region", "lat": 37.72585, "lon": -119.52926}, {"n": "Tenaya Canyon", "u": 0.45154, "v": 0.65245, "k": "region", "lat": 37.74074, "lon": -119.55934}, {"n": "Grand Canyon of the Tuolumne", "u": 0.53726, "v": 0.44274, "k": "region", "lat": 37.92687, "lon": -119.45877}, {"n": "Tuolumne Meadows", "u": 0.60691, "v": 0.49823, "k": "region", "lat": 37.87756, "lon": -119.37701}, {"n": "Glacier Point", "u": 0.43922, "v": 0.66369, "k": "region", "lat": 37.73076, "lon": -119.57377}, {"n": "Mariposa Grove", "u": 0.41313, "v": 0.90654, "k": "region", "lat": 37.51521, "lon": -119.60404}, {"n": "Tuolumne Grove", "u": 0.23786, "v": 0.62007, "k": "region", "lat": 37.76909, "lon": -119.8099}, {"n": "Merced Grove", "u": 0.21457, "v": 0.6422, "k": "region", "lat": 37.74937, "lon": -119.83712}, {"n": "Cathedral Range", "u": 0.64767, "v": 0.58445, "k": "region", "lat": 37.80098, "lon": -119.32932}, {"n": "Clark Range", "u": 0.58174, "v": 0.73779, "k": "region", "lat": 37.66498, "lon": -119.40683}, {"n": "The Minarets", "u": 0.78058, "v": 0.74249, "k": "region", "lat": 37.6604, "lon": -119.17404}, {"n": "Tioga Pass", "u": 0.70822, "v": 0.4603, "k": "region", "lat": 37.91104, "lon": -119.25793}, {"n": "Mono Pass", "u": 0.74625, "v": 0.5231, "k": "region", "lat": 37.85521, "lon": -119.21348}, {"n": "Bloody Canyon", "u": 0.81987, "v": 0.47816, "k": "region", "lat": 37.89485, "lon": -119.12685}, {"n": "Dana Plateau", "u": 0.73869, "v": 0.4502, "k": "region", "lat": 37.91993, "lon": -119.2221}, {"n": "Kerrick Canyon", "u": 0.39972, "v": 0.31642, "k": "region", "lat": 38.03892, "lon": -119.62056}, {"n": "Emigrant Basin", "u": 0.3765, "v": 0.1281, "k": "region", "lat": 38.20603, "lon": -119.64823}, {"n": "Bodie Hills", "u": 0.85234, "v": 0.09514, "k": "region", "lat": 38.23465, "lon": -119.08681}, {"n": "Devils Postpile", "u": 0.85953, "v": 0.78274, "k": "region", "lat": 37.62438, "lon": -119.0818}], "tours": [{"id": "hetchhetchy", "name": "Hetch Hetchy", "blurb": "A second Yosemite on the sheets, a reservoir in the terrain \u2014 the valley San Francisco was given in 1913.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.94734, "lon": -119.78871, "d": 8, "az": 95, "el": 15, "cap": "Hetch Hetchy Valley, drawn as a valley. The 1909 Yosemite quadrangle shows the Tuolumne meandering through a mile-wide floor of meadow and oak \u2014 the granite twin John Muir called 'one of Nature's rarest and most precious mountain temples.'"}, {"lat": 37.96417, "lon": -119.77278, "d": 5, "az": 200, "el": 14, "cap": "Tueeulala and Wapama falls on the north wall \u2014 Hetch Hetchy's Bridalveil and Yosemite Falls. Wapama runs all year, Tueeulala dries by midsummer; both now land in the reservoir."}, {"lat": 37.94585, "lon": -119.73655, "d": 9, "az": 270, "el": 16, "cap": "Pull the slider to the 1930 park map and the reservoir appears, drawn in hatched blue 'from approximate information only.' The Raker Act passed in December 1913; O'Shaughnessy Dam closed the gorge in 1923 and was raised in 1938."}, {"lat": 37.95492, "lon": -119.71045, "d": 7, "az": 250, "el": 15, "cap": "Rancheria Falls, at the head of the flooded reach. The Central Sierra Miwok and Northern Paiute burned and harvested this valley floor for its oaks and grass; 'Hetch Hetchy' is their word for a grass seed gathered here."}]}, {"id": "valley", "name": "The Valley", "blurb": "Seven square miles that started the whole idea \u2014 and the people who were driven out of them in 1851.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.7184, "lon": -119.682, "d": 9, "az": 70, "el": 13, "cap": "Into Yosemite Valley from the west, the way the Mariposa Battalion rode in March 1851 \u2014 a state-funded militia sent to force the Ahwahneechee out of their homeland. They burned the villages and the acorn caches; the people were marched to the Fresno River reservation, and Chief Tenaya's son was killed the next year."}, {"lat": 37.74213, "lon": -119.63589, "d": 6, "az": 110, "el": 12, "cap": "El Capitan, 3,000 feet of unbroken granite. On the 1909 sheet its contours crowd into a single brown stripe \u2014 the engraver simply ran out of room between 4,000 and 7,000 feet."}, {"lat": 37.7444, "lon": -119.53405, "d": 7, "az": 250, "el": 14, "cap": "Half Dome, 8,809 feet, cut by joints rather than by ice \u2014 Matthes showed in 1930 that the glaciers never overtopped it. George Anderson drilled and roped the back in 1875."}, {"lat": 37.75826, "lon": -119.59517, "d": 6, "az": 190, "el": 13, "cap": "Yosemite Falls, 2,425 feet in three leaps \u2014 the tallest on the continent. Lincoln signed the Yosemite Grant on 30 June 1864, handing the Valley and the Mariposa Grove to California; the state gave them back in 1906."}]}, {"id": "tioga", "name": "The Tioga Road", "blurb": "A silver company's wagon road over the crest, bought and given away \u2014 and the meadows the sheep ate first.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.87756, "lon": -119.37701, "d": 10, "az": 210, "el": 15, "cap": "Tuolumne Meadows, 8,600 feet \u2014 the largest subalpine meadow in the Sierra. Muir herded sheep through here in 1869 and spent the rest of his life calling them 'hoofed locusts'; the cavalry that ran the park after 1891 spent most summers chasing herders out."}, {"lat": 37.93743, "lon": -119.26043, "d": 6, "az": 220, "el": 14, "cap": "Bennettville and the Great Sierra Mine at 10,761 feet. The Great Sierra Consolidated Silver Company drove 1,800 feet of tunnel and built the 56-mile Great Sierra Wagon Road in 1882\u201383; the ore never paid, and the company was gone by 1884."}, {"lat": 37.91104, "lon": -119.25793, "d": 7, "az": 270, "el": 15, "cap": "Tioga Pass, 9,944 feet \u2014 the highest road crossing of the Sierra. Stephen Mather and friends bought the abandoned mining road in 1915 and handed it to the government; the 1930 park map is the first of these layers to draw it as a road for cars."}, {"lat": 37.8312, "lon": -119.46058, "d": 8, "az": 120, "el": 14, "cap": "Tenaya Lake, which the battalion named for the chief whose people had been living beside it. He told them it already had a name \u2014 Py-we-ack, the lake of the shining rocks."}]}, {"id": "crest", "name": "The Crest", "blurb": "Lyell, Ritter and the Minarets \u2014 the last living ice in the park, and elevations that were 85 feet wrong.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.73941, "lon": -119.27189, "d": 9, "az": 340, "el": 16, "cap": "Mount Lyell, the park's highest. The 1901 sheet prints 13,090 at the triangulation station and stipples the Lyell Glacier in blue beside it; the modern figure for the summit is 13,074. Next door the 1909 Yosemite sheet is blunter still, admitting in its own margin that its elevations \u2018were later found to be 85 feet too high.\u2019"}, {"lat": 37.74299, "lon": -119.26904, "d": 6, "az": 30, "el": 14, "cap": "The Lyell Glacier under the north face \u2014 measured by Israel Russell in 1883 and by the park ever since. It has lost most of its ice and stopped moving; the contours here record a glacier that no longer flows."}, {"lat": 37.68897, "lon": -119.19957, "d": 9, "az": 300, "el": 17, "cap": "Mount Ritter and Banner Peak. Muir climbed Ritter alone in October 1872 and froze on the face until, he wrote, 'a new sense of power' took over his hands and feet."}, {"lat": 37.6604, "lon": -119.17404, "d": 8, "az": 190, "el": 16, "cap": "The Minarets \u2014 volcanic slate stood on end. The 1901 sheet draws them as a serrated ridge with no names; Clarence King's survey had already been through, and the naming came later."}]}, {"id": "bodie", "name": "Bodie & Mono", "blurb": "Over the crest into the rain shadow: an alkali sea, a gold camp of 8,000, and the obsidian road between them.", "keys": [{"lat": 38.21214, "lon": -119.01209, "d": 7, "az": 250, "el": 14, "cap": "Bodie, at 8,379 feet. W. S. Bodey found gold here in 1859 and died in a blizzard that winter; the Standard Company's 1876 strike turned the camp into a town of thousands with its own Chinatown and two daily papers. The 1911 Bridgeport sheet still letters it in full, stamp mills and all."}, {"lat": 38.02742, "lon": -119.24154, "d": 7, "az": 110, "el": 15, "cap": "Lundy, in its canyon under the May Lundy Mine \u2014 gold found in 1879, a mill and a wagon road by 1881, and winters that ran avalanches down both walls onto the town."}, {"lat": 38.0076, "lon": -119.04959, "d": 14, "az": 270, "el": 14, "cap": "Mono Lake, three times as salty as the sea and at least 760,000 years old \u2014 one of the oldest lakes on the continent. Its Kutzadika'a Paiute name means 'fly eaters': the pupae of the alkali fly were harvested by the bushel and traded west over the passes."}, {"lat": 37.85521, "lon": -119.21348, "d": 8, "az": 280, "el": 16, "cap": "Mono Pass, 10,623 feet \u2014 the Mono Trail. Obsidian from the craters south of the lake went west over this notch, and acorns came east; the 1901 sheet draws it as a trail because it always was one."}]}], "mines": [{"n": "Bridalveil Fall", "u": 0.37685, "v": 0.67957, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Chilnualna Fall", "u": 0.40368, "v": 0.85175, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Illilouette Falls", "u": 0.4494, "v": 0.68279, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Lower Yosemite Fall", "u": 0.4198, "v": 0.64051, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Nevada Fall", "u": 0.47335, "v": 0.66997, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Rainbow Falls", "u": 0.85822, "v": 0.80841, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Rancheria Falls", "u": 0.32313, "v": 0.41093, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Ribbon Fall", "u": 0.3757, "v": 0.65798, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Sentinel Fall", "u": 0.41977, "v": 0.67024, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Tueeulala Falls", "u": 0.27014, "v": 0.40037, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Upper Yosemite Falls", "u": 0.41957, "v": 0.63488, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Vernal Fall", "u": 0.46274, "v": 0.66596, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Wapama Falls", "u": 0.27638, "v": 0.39797, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Waterwheel Falls", "u": 0.53725, "v": 0.44274, "c": "falls", "f": 1}, {"n": "Yosemite Falls",